# `for (..) s = (s + list[i] + t) | 0` with `t: number` from in front of the loop. The sum is cut to an int32, so it may be made in integers where every addend is one that fits.
# `t` was held as a double: the generator turned it into an integer and asked whether that was exact IN EVERY TURN (six instructions), or left the whole sum in doubles: fifteen cycles a turn from one sum to the next.
# A number from in front of the loop that a turn adds or takes away under a bit operator is asked for an int32 once, in front of the typed copy, and a double is held there as a number made out of its int32 word: whoever cuts the sum finds the word.
# Only what cannot hold a fraction by its type is asked: `(position + drift) | 0` is how a sum with a fraction is cut, and a test that fails at every entry would cost the whole typed copy. In `any` the number may be a fraction, so nothing is asked of it. In `scaled` it is multiplied, not added.
# -- source: a-number-from-in-front-of-the-loop-that-a-turn-adds-under-a-cut-to-an-int32-is-asked-for-one-in-front.ts
